ARCH_QA = [
("I need a full kill chain for compromising an enterprise Active Directory environment from initial access through domain compromise.","Full AD kill chain: Phase 1 — Initial Access: spearphish with macro-enabled Office doc or HTML smuggling (drops Cobalt Strike/Havoc beacon). Alternatively exploit internet-facing service (Citrix, Exchange ProxyShell, VPN). Phase 2 — Establish Foothold: deploy C2 beacon with HTTPS to legit-looking domain (categorized, aged). Run from memory only (process injection). Phase 3 — Local Recon: whoami /all, net user, ipconfig /all, systeminfo. Check for AV: tasklist /svc, sc query. Phase 4 — Privilege Escalation: PowerUp/PEASS to identify misconfigs. Common wins: unquoted service paths, weak service permissions, token impersonation (Incognito/SweetPotato), CVE-2021-1675 PrintNightmare, CVE-2022-21999 SpoolFool, Juicy/Rogue/Sweet Potato for SeImpersonatePrivilege. Phase 5 — Credential Access: Mimikatz sekurlsa::logonpasswords for LSASS dump (or SilentProcessExit technique to dump LSASS without touching it directly). Kerberoasting: Rubeus kerberoast /outfile:hashes.txt, crack with hashcat -m 13100. AS-REP roasting: Rubeus asreproast. Phase 6 — Lateral Movement: Pass-the-Hash with wmiexec/psexec, Pass-the-Ticket with captured Kerberos TGT. BloodHound for shortest path to Domain Admin. Phase 7 — Domain Compromise: DCSync (Mimikatz lsadump::dcsync /domain:corp.local /all) dumps all hashes. Golden Ticket: forge TGT with KRBTGT hash. Phase 8 — Persistence: skeleton key, DCShadow, adding backdoor admin account, deploying implant via Group Policy."),
("Explain BloodHound and how to use it to find the fastest path to Domain Admin.","BloodHound maps Active Directory relationships and attack paths using graph theory. Setup: deploy SharpHound collector (C# ingestor) on compromised host: SharpHound.exe -c All,GPOLocalGroup --zipfilename data.zip. Import ZIP to BloodHound. Key queries: 'Find Shortest Paths to Domain Admins' — automatically calculates all attack paths using Dijkstra's algorithm. Useful prebuilt queries: 'Find Principals with DCSync Rights', 'Find Computers where Domain Users are Local Admin', 'Shortest Paths from Owned Principals'. Mark compromised hosts/users as owned with right-click. BloodHound shows the exact attack chain: User A has GenericAll over Group B → Group B has WriteDACL on OU C → OU C contains Domain Admin account. Abuse relationships: GenericAll/GenericWrite over user → set SPN (Kerberoast), change password. WriteDACL → grant self DCSync rights. AllowedToDelegate → constrained delegation attack. AllowedToAct → resource-based constrained delegation. ForceChangePassword → change password. Admin rights on computer → dump LSASS. BloodHound 4.0 added Azure/Entra ID attack paths — same graph theory applied to cloud."),
("Walk through a Kerberoasting attack from detection to cracking to usage.","Kerberoasting targets service accounts with SPNs (Service Principal Names) registered in AD. Any domain user can request a Kerberos service ticket (TGS) for any SPN — the TGS is encrypted with the service account's NTLM hash. This is by design and generates no alert without proper monitoring. Step 1 — Enumerate SPNs: setspn -T domain -Q */* from Windows, or from Linux: GetUserSPNs.py domain/user:password -dc-ip DC_IP. Step 2 — Request TGS: Rubeus kerberoast /outfile:hashes.txt (requests TGS for all SPNs). impacket: GetUserSPNs.py domain/user:password -dc-ip DC_IP -request -outputfile hashes.txt. Step 3 — Crack: hashcat -m 13100 hashes.txt rockyou.txt --rules-file best64.rule. Target service accounts often have weak passwords (set years ago, never changed, not in scope for password policies). Step 4 — Use cracked credentials: net use \\server\share /user:domain\serviceaccount password. If service account is local admin on servers: wmiexec.py, psexec.py. If service account has DCSync rights (common for SQL service accounts): Mimikatz lsadump::dcsync. Detection: Windows Event ID 4769 (TGS request) with encryption type 0x17 (RC4) from non-service account. Mitigation: use gMSA (group Managed Service Accounts), enforce AES-only Kerberos."),
("What is Pass-the-Hash and how do you use it for lateral movement?","Pass-the-Hash (PtH) uses a captured NTLM hash to authenticate without knowing the plaintext password. NTLM authentication doesn't require the actual password — the hash IS the credential. Capture hashes: Mimikatz sekurlsa::logonpasswords dumps hashes from LSASS memory. Requires local admin/SYSTEM. Alternative: reg save HKLM\\SYSTEM system.hive && reg save HKLM\\SAM sam.hive → secretsdump.py -sam sam.hive -system system.hive LOCAL. Tools: impacket suite (Linux): wmiexec.py -hashes LM:NTLM domain/user@target. psexec.py -hashes LM:NTLM domain/user@target cmd.exe. smbexec.py, atexec.py, dcomexec.py — all accept hashes. Cobalt Strike: pth command. CrackMapExec: cme smb 192.168.1.0/24 -u admin -H NTLM_HASH --local-auth to spray hash across subnet. Winrm: evil-winrm -i target -u user -H hash. RDP: xfreerdp /u:user /pth:NTLM_HASH /v:target (requires restricted admin mode enabled). Limitations: only works for NTLM auth (not Kerberos). Mitigation: Credential Guard virtualizes LSASS, prevents NTLM hash extraction from memory. Local admin account disabled (disables SMB lateral movement)."),
("How does DCSync work and what can you do with the KRBTGT hash?","DCSync mimics a Domain Controller's replication protocol (MS-DRSR) to request password data for any account including KRBTGT — the master Kerberos ticket-signing account. Requirements: Replicating Directory Changes and Replicating Directory Changes All rights — granted to Domain Admins, Enterprise Admins, and any account explicitly granted these permissions. Command: Mimikatz lsadump::dcsync /domain:corp.local /user:krbtgt — dumps KRBTGT NT hash and previous hash. With impacket: secretsdump.py domain/admin:password@DC_IP -just-dc-user krbtgt. KRBTGT hash usage — Golden Ticket attack: Mimikatz kerberos::golden /domain:corp.local /sid:S-1-5-21-xxx /krbtgt:HASH /user:Administrator /id:500 /groups:512 /ticket:golden.kirbi. This forges a valid TGT for any user with any group memberships. kerberos::ptt golden.kirbi loads the ticket. Now authenticated as Domain Admin on any machine. Golden tickets survive password resets — valid until KRBTGT password changed TWICE. Persistence value: even after incident response and password resets, if KRBTGT not changed (requires two changes 10 hours apart), Golden Ticket remains valid. Detection: Event ID 4769 with unusually high privilege, Mimikatz signatures in memory."),
("Explain Active Directory Certificate Services (ADCS) attacks — ESC1 through ESC8.","ADCS attack techniques (Will Schroeder, Lee Christensen — 'Certified Pre-Owned'): ESC1 — Enrollee Supplies Subject: Certificate template allows requestor to specify Subject Alternative Name (SAN). Any user can request cert with SAN = Administrator@domain → use cert to authenticate as Domain Admin. certutil or certipy: certipy req -username user@domain -password pass -ca CA_NAME -template TEMPLATE -upn administrator@domain. ESC2 — Any Purpose EKU: Template usable for any purpose including client auth. ESC3 — Enrollment Agent: Template allows requesting certs on behalf of other users. ESC4 — Vulnerable Template ACL: Low-priv user has write access to template — modify template to ESC1 conditions. ESC5 — Vulnerable PKI Object ACL: Control over CA object or ADCS server itself. ESC6 — EDITF_ATTRIBUTESUBJECTALTNAME2: CA flag allows SAN in any request. ESC7 — Vulnerable CA ACL: ManageCA right allows enabling ESC6 flag. ESC8 — NTLM Relay to AD CS HTTP Endpoints: Coerce DC authentication (PetitPotam, PrinterBug), relay NTLM to ADCS web enrollment → get DC certificate → DCSync via Kerberos. Certipy tool automates all ESC variants: certipy find -username user@domain -password pass -dc-ip DC_IP for enumeration."),
("How do you conduct a red team engagement from scoping to debrief?","Full red team engagement lifecycle: Scoping: define objectives (domain admin, data exfil, physical access), authorized IP ranges, off-limits systems (production DBs), rules of engagement (working hours, emergency stop word), engagement window. Planning: threat model — which APT group TTPs to emulate? MITRE ATT&CK navigator for planning coverage. OSINT phase — passive recon of target, attack surface mapping. Infrastructure: C2 infrastructure setup (categorized domains, valid SSL, legitimate-looking hostnames), redirectors, payload hosting, C2 profile (Malleable C2 for CS to blend with legitimate traffic). Initial Access phase: phishing campaign (GoPhish), exploitation of external vulnerabilities, physical intrusion/tailgating if in scope. Internal ops: escalate privileges, move laterally, establish persistence, achieve objectives. Document everything: timestamps, commands, screenshots, C2 logs — evidence chain for report. Exfiltration simulation: exfil test data over DNS/HTTPS to prove data could leave network. Debrief: notify blue team immediately after completion, provide IoCs from the engagement, walk through attack chain in Purple Team review, produce written report: Executive Summary (business impact), Technical Narrative (step-by-step), Findings (each vulnerability), Remediation Roadmap (prioritized by risk)."),
("What are the most effective initial access techniques in 2024-2025?","Current highest-yield initial access vectors: 1) Phishing with HTML smuggling: HTML file containing encoded payload decoded by JavaScript on client side — bypasses email attachment scanning. Drops ISO/ZIP containing LNK file. 2) Microsoft Teams phishing: External tenants can message any Teams user. Pretend to be IT support, send malicious link. No email security gateway. 3) Browser-in-the-Browser (BitB): fake browser popup that looks like legitimate OAuth window — captures SSO credentials. 4) SEO poisoning: malicious files ranked for searches like 'download advanced IP scanner' or 'free adobe premiere download'. 5) Malvertising: legitimate ad networks serving malicious JavaScript. 6) QR code phishing (Quishing): bypass email security that doesn't process QR code destinations. 7) PDF with embedded link: security gateways often don't follow PDF links. 8) Callback phishing (vishing): email says 'call this number about your account' → phone operator does real-time social engineering and AnyDesk/TeamViewer install. 9) EvilProxy/Evilginx campaigns against O365: bypass MFA. 10) Supply chain: compromise a trusted software vendor's update mechanism. 11) Exploit internet-facing services: CitrixBleed (CVE-2023-4966), Ivanti (CVE-2024-21887), PAN-OS (CVE-2024-3400)."),
("How does Mimikatz work and what are all its capabilities?","Mimikatz (Benjamin Delpy) is the canonical Windows credential extraction tool. Core modules: sekurlsa — interacts with LSASS memory: sekurlsa::logonpasswords (NTLM hashes + cleartext passwords), sekurlsa::wdigest (cleartext passwords — disabled in Win8.1+, can re-enable via registry), sekurlsa::kerberos (Kerberos tickets), sekurlsa::msv (MSV hashes), sekurlsa::dpapi (DPAPI master keys). lsadump — interacts with SAM/NTDS: lsadump::sam (local SAM database), lsadump::dcsync (replication API to dump any AD account), lsadump::lsa /patch (patch LSASS to dump all domain hashes), lsadump::cache (cached domain credentials). kerberos — ticket operations: kerberos::list (list tickets), kerberos::ptt (pass-the-ticket), kerberos::golden (forge golden ticket), kerberos::silver (forge silver ticket for specific service). crypto — certificate operations, DPAPI: dpapi::cred (decrypt DPAPI-protected credentials), dpapi::chrome (Chrome saved passwords). privilege — token manipulation: privilege::debug (get SeDebugPrivilege). misc — misc functions: misc::skeleton (skeleton key backdoor patches KDC). Execution: requires SYSTEM or SeDebugPrivilege for most operations. OPSEC: invoke-mimikatz loads from memory, avoids disk. Modern EDRs detect via process hollowing and direct LSASS access patterns."),
("Explain threat hunting methodology and how to hunt for living-off-the-land attacks.","Threat hunting is proactive searching for threats that have bypassed automated detection. Methodology: Hypothesis-driven: start with specific TTP (e.g., 'Adversary using PowerShell for discovery') → hunt for evidence in logs. Intel-driven: current threat intel (ISAC, CISA advisories) drives hypothesis. TTP-based: MITRE ATT&CK — pick under-detected techniques. LotL (Living off the Land) hunting — adversaries use built-in Windows tools (PowerShell, WMI, WMIC, certutil, mshta, regsvr32, cscript, msiexec, rundll32): PowerShell: hunt for encoded commands (contains '-enc' or '-EncodedCommand'), unusual parent processes (Word, Outlook spawning PowerShell), execution from temp/user directories. WMIC: hunt for wmic process call create with network connections, wmic /node: (remote execution). Certutil: certutil -urlcache -split -f (file download) — hunt in process command lines. LOLBin abuse: regsvr32 /s /n /u /i: (Squiblydoo), mshta http://, rundll32 executing from unusual DLL paths. Data sources: Windows Event Logs (4688 with command line, 4104 PowerShell script block logging, 4624/4625 auth, 7045 new service), Sysmon (process creation with full command line, network connections, file creation), EDR telemetry. Tooling: Sigma rules for SIEM, YARA for file-based hunting, Velociraptor for enterprise-scale artifact collection."),
]

VIRELLE_QA = [
("Explain the three-act structure and its variations used in Hollywood screenwriting.","The three-act structure is the foundational architecture of Western narrative film. Act 1 (Setup, ~25% runtime): Establish ordinary world, introduce protagonist, inciting incident disrupts ordinary world, protagonist makes decision at plot point 1 that ends Act 1. Act 2 (Confrontation, ~50%): protagonist pursues goal against escalating obstacles, midpoint shifts direction or raises stakes significantly, dark night of the soul / all-is-lost moment near end of act, plot point 2 launches into Act 3. Act 3 (Resolution, ~25%): climax confrontation, resolution of all narrative threads, denouement. Variations: Save the Cat (Blake Snyder) breaks this into 15 beats with precise page counts — more formulaic but useful for commercial scripts. Five-act structure (TV drama — Breaking Bad, The Wire): each act ends on a hook, allows more breathing room for character development. Non-linear structures: in medias res opening (Memento, Pulp Fiction), frame narratives, multiple timelines. The Hero's Journey (Campbell/Vogler) maps mythological structure over three acts. Anti-structure: European art cinema often rejects clear act breaks (Antonioni, Tarkovsky) — character revelation replaces plot mechanics. Genre modifies the structure: horror inciting incident comes earlier, comedy has strict set-piece requirements at specific beats."),
("What makes Kubrick's visual language distinct and how did he achieve it?","Stanley Kubrick's visual grammar is defined by mathematical precision, psychological geometry, and a cold observational gaze. Key elements: Symmetrical composition: Kubrick used perfect bilateral symmetry extensively (the Overlook Hotel corridors, Alex's bedroom in Clockwork Orange, the war room in Dr. Strangelove) — creates unease and artificial order that suggests forces beyond human control. One-point perspective: camera placed on central axis with converging lines leading to vanishing point (the axe chase corridor, the hedge maze, military barracks in Full Metal Jacket) — simultaneously draws viewer in and creates claustrophobic compression. Kubrick zoom: slow forward dolly combined with slow zoom out (or vice versa) creates 'vertigo' effect where background and foreground move at different rates — used in Barry Lyndon and The Shining. Barry Lyndon's natural light cinematography: Kubrick used NASA-developed f/0.7 lenses modified by Zeiss to shoot entirely by candlelight — 18th century paintings brought to life. Long Steadicam tracking shots (Shining — OB-WAN scenes): immersive but slightly wrong, creating dread through fluid pursuit. Kubrick's obsessive research translated into productions that took years — Full Metal Jacket shot over 400 days. His visual choices always served psychological theme: the perfect compositions represent the dehumanizing forces (war, capitalism, madness) his films examine."),
("Explain cinematography lens choices — focal length, depth of field, and psychological effect.","Focal length fundamentally changes how a viewer perceives space and subjects: Wide angle (14-35mm): Exaggerates perspective depth, foreground appears larger relative to background, slight distortion at extremes. Psychological effect: expands space, creates environment emphasis, at extreme wide angles (8-14mm) creates distortion that suggests psychological distortion or forced perspective (Citizen Kane's deep focus, Dr. Caligari expressionist influence). Used for chase sequences, action, establishing expansive environments. Normal (40-60mm): Closest to human eye perception, no distortion, natural space. Creates documentary realism (Dardenne brothers, Dogme 95). Psychological neutrality. Telephoto (85-400mm+): Compresses space, makes foreground and background appear at similar distance, shallow depth of field. 85-105mm: flattering for portraiture, slight compression. 135mm: Gordon Willis's 'Prince of Darkness' lens — Godfather shots that isolate subjects. 300-400mm: surveillance feeling, celebrity paparazzi quality. Used by Vilmos Zsigmond for McCabe & Mrs. Miller — telephoto through snowstorms creates impressionistic distance. Depth of field: shallow DoF (wide aperture) isolates subject from background, directs attention, intimate or voyeuristic. Deep DoF (stop down) — Citizen Kane's revolutionary deep focus (Gregg Toland, f/3.5 throughout with composite mattes) — all planes in focus simultaneously, camera doesn't need to reveal what's important."),
("What is the cinematographic philosophy of Roger Deakins and how does light define his work?","Roger Deakins ASC BSC is widely considered the greatest living cinematographer. His philosophy centers on motivated lighting — every light source in frame should appear to come from a real-world source (window, lamp, fire, screen), even when artificial. Key techniques: Practicals integration: Deakins obsesses over practical light sources in frame. The prisoners' flames in Shawshank, the office fluorescents in Blade Runner 2049, the burning windmill in True Grit. The practical light motivates the artificial light that actually illuminates the scene. No Lights approach: on 1917, Deakins and director Sam Mendes designed the production so that natural light was maximized — they built the trenches and environments to allow natural light to work. Collaboration with production design: Deakins always works closely with PD to ensure practical sources are positioned correctly for camera. Blade Runner 2049 visual language: Deakins and Denis Villeneuve agreed on a color-coded world — each location has a distinct color palette (orange dust, white snow, grey city). Lighting never decorative — always expresses the psychological state of characters. 1917 one-shot technique: demanded extremely precise lighting design since camera couldn't stop. Mixed practicals (natural light, fire torches) with pre-rigged LED panels hidden in environment. His career spans Coen Brothers (every film since Barton Fink), No Country for Old Men's stark naturalism, Shawshank's warmth, Sicario's bleached-out border violence."),
("Explain the visual storytelling language of color in cinema.","Color in film functions as an emotional subtext layer operating below conscious narrative. Primary approaches: Symbolic color coding: specific colors carry consistent meaning within a film's world (not universal — culturally and contextually determined). Red in Schindler's List: the girl's red coat is the only color in the black-and-white film — isolates innocence against systematic extermination. Blue in Moonlight: coolness, beauty, outside world, sexual awakening — cinematographer James Laxton's blue-green palette vs warm skin tones. Color arc / transformation: protagonist's color association changes as character changes. In Amélie (Bruno Delbonnel): warm amber-yellow world of Paris reflects Amélie's enchanted perspective — when she withdraws, cooler tones. In Three Colors trilogy (Kieslowski + Slawomir Idziak/Piotr Sobocinski): Blue/White/Red directly embody the concepts they explore. Desaturation for realism or psychological withdrawal: Saving Private Ryan's desaturated, bleached-out palette (Janusz Kaminski) — war drains color from life. Traffic's three color-coded storylines each have distinct palette — green for Mexico, yellow-grey for US drug plot, blue for suburban story. Complementary color contrast: orange/teal (ubiquitous in Hollywood blockbusters via DI grading — warm skin tones against teal shadows). Production design + costume + lighting must coordinate for cohesive color language. Director of Photography and production designer must establish color bible in pre-production."),
("What separates great screenwriting dialogue from average dialogue?","Great cinematic dialogue serves character revelation, subtext, and plot simultaneously while sounding natural — a paradox since people in real life rarely speak with such precision. Character revelation through diction: every character should have a distinct voice determined by their education, background, psychological state, desire. Mamet's characters speak in clipped, rhythmic patterns that reveal status games and power dynamics. The Coen Brothers' characters use hyper-specific regional speech (Fargo's Minnesotan cadences, No Country's sparse Texas reticence). Subtext — saying one thing meaning another: professional screenplay dialogue rarely states emotional content directly. Characters talk around what they want. In Before Sunrise, Celine and Jesse discuss other things while conducting the most important conversation of their lives. In Inherent Vice, the comedy comes from characters who speak elliptically around paranoid realities. 'No dialogue should mean exactly what it says' — the deeper meaning creates dramatic irony when audience understands what characters are avoiding. Conflict embedded: every scene of dialogue should have competing wants — each character wants something different from the conversation. The dialogue is the battlefield. Economical exposition: bad dialogue = 'As you know, Bob, we are scientists working on a top-secret project.' Good exposition is embedded in conflict, character behavior, or visual storytelling — the dialogue doesn't explain what the film can show. Specificity over generality: 'a 1967 Chevrolet Impala' over 'a car.' Specific details create world."),
("How does film editing shape emotion and meaning — explain the core principles.","Editing is the final rewrite — the editor determines what a film is. Core principles: Kuleshov Effect: the meaning of a shot changes based on what precedes and follows it. Pudovkin's experiments showed the same actor's face read as hunger, grief, or joy depending on adjacent shots. This is cinema's unique grammar. Cut on action: matching action across a cut creates continuity — the audience's eye follows movement across the edit, perceiving continuous space. Jump cut: Godard in Breathless made jump cuts famous — cuts within the same shot that violate continuity. Creates disorientation, energy, self-consciousness. L-cuts and J-cuts: audio leads or lags video — dialogue from next scene plays over image of current scene (L-cut), or vice versa (J-cut). Creates fluid transitions, bridges emotional states. Intercutting / cross-cutting: parallel editing between simultaneous events creates tension (Griffith's last-minute rescue), thematic juxtaposition (the christening / murders in Godfather), irony (Eisenstein's October). Rhythm: cut timing creates tempo — fast cuts in action create excitement, held shots create unease or contemplation. Tarkovsky holds shots beyond comfort. The Revenant's editing rhythm mimics trauma — disorienting, unpredictable. Invisible editing ideal: classical Hollywood wanted editing to be unnoticed. Continuity editing rules (180-degree rule, match on action) exist to maintain this illusion. Breaking rules draws attention to the medium itself — Godard, Bergman, Bresson used visible editing as statement."),
("What is the Dogme 95 movement and what did it contribute to cinema?","Dogme 95 was a radical filmmaking manifesto created by Lars von Trier and Thomas Vinterberg in Copenhagen in 1995. The 'Vow of Chastity' had 10 rules: shooting on location (no artificial sets), diegetic sound only (no score added in post), handheld camera, color film (no special lighting), no genre conventions (murder, weapons), no superficial action (no stunts), no temporal/spatial alienation, no director credit on film. Philosophy: strip cinema of artifice and illusion to expose raw truth. Counter-reaction against Hollywood spectacle and auteur cinema's fetishism of visual style. Key films: Festen (Vinterberg, 1998) — filmed on early consumer DV camera at a family celebration where abuse is revealed. The Celebration's raw DV texture made the psychological exposure feel documentary. Idioterne (von Trier, 1998) — deliberately provocative. Impact on cinema: democratized filmmaking — DV cameras meant anyone could make a Dogme film. Influenced the entire movement toward digital naturalism. Direct precursor to: Mumblecore movement (Swanberg, Duplass brothers), contemporary naturalistic acting styles, the 'dogmatic' approaches of Dardenne brothers (though predating Dogme). The rules were eventually violated by the founders themselves — Dogme was more polemical provocation than lasting constraint. Its real contribution: proved that technical limitation breeds creative freedom and that emotional truth could overwhelm technical polish."),
]

def write_dataset(path, qa_list, sys_msg):
    with open(path, "w") as f:
        for q, a in qa_list:
            entry = {"messages": [
                {"role": "system", "content": sys_msg},
                {"role": "user", "content": q},
                {"role": "assistant", "content": a}
            ]}
            f.write(json.dumps(entry, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    print(f"Wrote {len(qa_list)} entries to {path}")

write_dataset(f"{OUT}/archibald_cybersecurity_expanded.jsonl", ARCH_QA, SYS_ARCH)
write_dataset(f"{OUT}/virelle_cinema_expanded.jsonl", VIRELLE_QA, SYS_VIR)
print("Script 4 DONE")
